{
  "teamId": 141,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Toronto Blue Jays entered the season with playoff aspirations but quickly revealed themselves to be a team in transition. The opening weeks offered genuine reason for optimism — Justin Smoak launched a grand slam on Opening Day, Russell Martin went deep the very next game, and Curtis Granderson provided spark after spark in April. The Blue Jays went 15-10 in April, a promising start that masked deeper structural problems lurking beneath the surface.\n\nMay shattered those illusions. The bullpen, a chronic vulnerability all season, became a catastrophe. Tyler Clippard surrendered a grand slam to Chad Pinder that swung the game by nearly 0.6 WPA, then allowed Shohei Ohtani to rip a decisive single against the Angels. The Jays went 9-19 in May, their worst month, and any talk of contention evaporated. The rotation was leaking runs, the bullpen was actively imploding, and the offense couldn't keep pace with a pitching staff allowing nearly five runs per game.\n\nJune and the first half of July offered a curious respite — the Blue Jays actually steadied at 14-12 in June, buoyed by clutch moments from Steve Pearce, Aledmys Díaz, and Kendrys Morales. But Ryan Tepera was victimized repeatedly in high-leverage spots, including a brutal walk-off homer by Alex Bregman, and the team dealt away veterans at the trade deadline, acknowledging reality. The second half of July and August became a steady grind of modest losing, punctuated by Yangervis Solarte's heroics and Kevin Pillar's occasional brilliance.\n\nThe season's most telling bookends came from Justin Smoak — he opened the year with a grand slam and closed the meaningful portion of the campaign with a bases-clearing grand slam on August 31 that stood as the single highest-leverage positive play of the year. By September, with the roster turning over to younger faces like Lourdes Gurriel Jr. and Rowdy Tellez, the Blue Jays were playing out the string. A 73-89 record told the full story of a franchise at a crossroads, one that had scored plenty of runs and hit 217 home runs but surrendered 832, victims of a pitching staff that simply could not hold leads when it mattered most.",
